1. Token Expiry Not Enforced After Handshake
Current: The JWT is verified only during the Socket.IO handshake. Once connected, the socket stays alive indefinitely, even when the JWT has long expired (15min TTL).

Impact: A socket authenticated at T=0 remains valid at T=∞. If the user's session is revoked server-side, they keep receiving real-time events.

2. No In-Band Token Refresh
Current: When the client's token expires and @novyse/auth refreshes it (via POST /token), the socket has no way to know. The client code in 
socket-io.ts:135-141
 does a full disconnect -> reconnect on socketReconnect:

```javascript
eventEmitter.getEmitter().on("socketReconnect", async () => {
  if (socket) socket.disconnect();
  await SocketIO.open();
});
```
Impact: Every token refresh triggers a WebSocket teardown, new TCP handshake, new JWKS verification, new Hasura query for all chat rooms, and re-registration of all event listeners. This causes ~1-3 seconds of real-time blindness per refresh cycle (every ~15 minutes).

3. No Session Revocation Channel
Current: There is no Redis channel or mechanism for the authentication service to signal "session X has been revoked" to Socket.IO. If a user logs out from one device, other active sockets for that session remain connected.

Impact: Logout is not instant across devices. The socket persists until the next reconnection attempt fails.

4. connect_error Handling is Fragile
Current: The client checks error.message.includes("Authentication error") or error.message.includes("jwt expired") via string matching:

```javascript
socket.on("connect_error", async (error) => {
  if (error.message.includes("Authentication error") || 
      error.message.includes("jwt expired")) {
    socket.disconnect();
    setTimeout(() => SocketIO.open(), 1000);
  }
});
```
Impact: Fragile, if error messages change, the client loses its ability to differentiate auth errors from network errors. No structured error codes.
